# The packages

One source, three binary packages, so that what the editor previews is what
the server produces:

- `python3-danu`: the `danu` Python package, which both of the others run.
- `danu-server`: the pipeline, installed the way a package should be - entry
  points in `/usr/bin`, configuration in `/etc/danu`, data in `/var/lib/danu`,
  rebuildable working directories in `/var/cache/danu`, units in
  `/usr/lib/systemd/system`.
- `danu-editor`: the editor, `danu` in `/usr/bin`, on the distribution's
  PySide6, with a desktop entry and its icon.

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

Until 0.2.0-2 the Python package was in `danu-server`; `python3-danu` breaks
and replaces the older `danu-server`, so an upgrade hands the files over.

`isofill` is a dependency rather than a bundled copy; it has its own package
built from its own repository. GDAL comes from the distribution for the same
reason it is absent from `pyproject.toml`: the PyPI builds are not worth
relying on.
